WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.475
Let’s read a real one together.

2
00:00:02.725 --> 00:00:06.000
The question says: what’s the position of this light?

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.125
Follow it on screen.

4
00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:12.400
Lay a straightedge level from the light across to the side scale.

5
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.400
It reads forty-one degrees, ten point two minutes north.

6
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.750
That’s the latitude done.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.825
Now longitude.

8
00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:28.200
With the dividers, measure from the nearest meridian, seventy-one degrees thirty minutes west, across to the light.

9
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.525
Carry that to the top scale.

10
00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:33.175
It spans four point six minutes.

11
00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:36.000
The light is west of that meridian.

12
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.575
So do we add the four point six, or subtract it?

13
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:41.875
Add.

14
00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.875
West longitude grows to the west.

15
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:48.975
Seventy-one degrees, thirty-four point six minutes west.

16
00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:56.000
So the answer is forty-one degrees ten point two north, seventy-one degrees thirty-four point six west.

17
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.150
That’s lesson two.

18
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.000
Latitude from the side, longitude from the top.

19
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.250
West grows to the left.

20
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.300
And latitude is always written first.

21
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.425
Next time: courses and distances.
